very finding below is **read**, not verified.\n\n1. **`trustedRef` runs two redundant `rev-parse` calls per candidate** (group `trusted-config`, `src/config.ts`, about 10 lines changed). The loop checks `refs/remotes/${b}` and `refs/heads/${b}` and then calls `merge-base HEAD b`. `gitTry` already returns null when `merge-base` can't resolve a name, so the two `rev-parse` calls can go. The loop becomes `for (const b of [...]) { const mb = gitTry(['merge-base','HEAD',b])?.trim(); if (mb) return mb }`. That is up to 8 fewer git spawns when `main` is missing. The explicit `refs/...` checks also guard against a tag named `main`. If you want that guard, keep a single `rev-parse --verify --quiet ${b}^{commit}` instead of the two.\n\n2. **`MAX_LINE` is exported but never used outside `safe-match.ts`** (group `safe-match`, `src/safe-match.ts:8`). Drop `export`. It would only be worth keeping if a test imported it, and the tests hard-code 5000 instead. This is a one-word change.\n\n3. **`matchLines` runs the vm once per hunk per pattern, and `texts` is re-sliced each time** (group `safe-match`, `src/signals.ts` h8 and `src/safe-match.ts:19`). With P patterns, this is P `runInContext` calls and P array copies per hunk. Slicing once in `hunkSignals`, where `texts` is built, would avoid the repeated copies. A bigger change would batch every hunk's lines per pattern, which is also what the \"skipped once\" comment assumes. I didn't measure it and the cost is probably small, so only the cheap slice move is worth doing.\n\n4. **The lint fixture test now builds a second repo through a `lint(withConfig)` closure** (group `tests`, `test/signals.test.ts` h14). The test rebuilds the whole 62-file repo twice to compare \"with config\" against \"without\". Calling `commitConfig` before the lint commit on a single repo would not work, because the first assertion needs the no-config result. The simpler form is `lint()` once, then run `signals` at a second ref, or use `r.g('checkout')` to the pre-config state. The current form is correct but heavier than it needs to be. Low priority.\n\n5. **The `loadConfig` comment wraps awkwardly** (group `trusted-config`, `src/config.ts`). \"Throws / ConfigError (with the file and key)…\" now breaks mid-sentence after the inserted parenthetical. Reflow it into one sentence, or move the base-branch note onto `readRepoConfig`, where it already appears in the `trustedRef` comment.\n\nApart from these, the change is lean. The vm budget and the base-branch read are small and well tested.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
