8e2f94934bb7b50","f939839f951a290c","fa29cea540e16e30","fa29cea540e16e30","fade43691fece55d","fc19e811751009b6","fc5a968d53ec2aa2","fd8bddf5946fed04","fd9ae7db6499f4b7"],"groups":["6e5370c494b0d8e7","59f6689768629487","4873f4fc8673de40","9aadd502ca3b05d5","5de7f6dd4d07f774","c32eda83e79b7b5a","6c973db6cee8ad63","664acfd686548a0f","9898c2321c8027dc","b18364236cf688c5","832f422e509de37c","a1eeac95e9383cc8"],"findings":"## Review of the config-hardening change\n\nTests and typecheck pass in the clone, and the core regex guard behaves as described. The problems are in the annotations (a summary claim the code doesn't back, and a task item that is only partly done) and in a few gaps the author did not list.\n\n**Verification:** `npm run typecheck` is clean. `npm test` passes 188/188 (log in `scratch/test.log`). A scratch probe under `scratch/` ran `matchLines` directly.\n\n### Mismatches\n\n1. **`safe-match` summary claims a line cap that does not exist. (verified, read)** The task summary says \"Regex matching runs under a vm time budget with a line cap\". `src/safe-match.ts` has no cap, and its own comment says \"Lines are never cut\". The evidence entry cites a test \"'a huge line is cut'\", but no such test exists in `test/signals.test.ts` (grep finds only \"cut off and skipped\"). The padding test that replaced it (`'x'.repeat(5000) + ' password'`) would also pass with no guard at all, so it does not test the behaviour it names.\n\n2. **Task item 2 is only partly done. (read)** The TODO item asks for two things: read config from a trusted ref, and \"show a warning when the change itself edits `.gloss/config.json`\". The author's `config-sensitive` group says \"rating only: no separate warning\". The task is framed as \"TODO.md security items 1-2\" and the summary implies both are done. The deferral is recorded in the new TODO.md lines, but the headline overstates completion.\n\n3. **`trustedRef` comment says \"the first ... that exists\"; the code takes the first that yields a merge-base. (read)** A ref that exists but shares no history with HEAD falls through to the next candidate. The comment and the docs in README should say that.\n\n### Misgraded necessity\n\n4. **`config-sensitive` is labelled `required` but is a rating-only change of one line in `DEFAULTS`.** It is needed for item 2's rating half, so `required` is defensible. Its evidence test does pass. But it is the only group that is not really \"the task\", and its \"if removed\" wording (\"would not be rated sensitive\") is accurate. I would keep it, with the caveat that it is not the warning the TODO asked for.\n\nNothing else looks misgraded. `safe-match` as `required` is right: a catastrophic pattern does stall the tool.\n\n### Removal candidates\n\nNone worth removing. The `tests` group is needed for the three behaviours, and the `docs` group is small.\n\n### Simplifications\n\n5. **The `ctx` sharing in `safe-match.ts` keeps `ctx.texts` referencing the last batch after the call returns. (read)** This is minor, but clearing `ctx.texts`/`ctx.re` after the run would avoid holding large arrays alive. Not worth a change on its own.\n\n### Missing risks\n\n6. **One timed-out batch disables that pattern for every later hunk in the run. (verified)** The probe shows the skip set is keyed by pattern source and is process-global. After a catastrophic input, a fresh call with the same source on an unrelated batch returns all `false`. The author documents \"skipped for the rest of the run\", but the security consequence is not stated: a change's own line can make a trusted pattern time out and silently drop hits on its other hunks. That is a path to lowering its own floor, which this change is meant to prevent. It needs a catastrophic pattern in the base config, so it is not reachable from a clean config, but it should be listed or mitigated. For example, the skip could be scoped per batch, or the floor could be marked as degraded.\n\n7. **Budget cost is not an issue for ordinary patterns. (verified)** A benign pattern over 300,000 lines finished in 40 ms, well under the 250 ms budget. The wall-clock nondeterminism risk is therefore limited to patterns near the budget, which the author already notes.\n\n8. **Trust is pinned to the merge-base, so a long-lived branch uses the config from its fork point. (read)** A tightening of the base config made after the branch forked is ignored until the branch is rebased or merged back. The author chose merge-base deliberately, but the docs should say that the reviewer sees the base's config as of the fork, not as of now.\n\n9. **Trust is anchored to the checked-out HEAD, not to the commit being reviewed. (read)** `trustedRef` calls `merge-base HEAD …`. When reviewing a commit range that is not the checked-out branch, the config comes from the checked-out branch's base. This is probably correct for a reviewer's own checkout, but it is not stated.\n\n10. **Repos whose default branch is not one of the four names get defaults and a warning on every run. (read)** The warning is printed once per process because of the cache, so it is not noisy, but it is a silent change of behaviour for those repos. The docs mention it.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
