2dea136d23a88","e53f7e684953d4e6","e5dcf4c676afe9a2","e5f5e95a1ced99d1","e631dcd699b8d8bf","e769ac588e46a4b6","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","ea6d1937747e010c","ea9eaaf74db8796b","ebae4cbe7ea22476","ec33a89f3eb1b3e1","ee795f4182eeccc7","f04af5bcb4a53925","f1a5828d279362bd","f32a7d88834a56f2","f72aea690ce12b9f","f7dcb919a01ce51e","f8e2f94934bb7b50","f939839f951a290c","fa29cea540e16e30","fa29cea540e16e30","fade43691fece55d","fc19e811751009b6","fc5a968d53ec2aa2","fd8bddf5946fed04"],"groups":["fec2a20e613652e6","503ed6368d8710f4","f0a78fd508b2f9a9","9aadd502ca3b05d5","5de7f6dd4d07f774","cc61f997d5d8cddd","6c973db6cee8ad63","664acfd686548a0f","9898c2321c8027dc","ca3ca833cde0540e","b18364236cf688c5","832f422e509de37c","a1eeac95e9383cc8"],"findings":"I found two defects. The first I confirmed by running it. The second I got from reading the code. I did not get a result from `npm test`: it passed the 120s foreground limit and was still running in the background when I stopped.\n\n**1. `mergeConfig` mutates the shared built-in defaults** (major)\n- **Where:** `src/config.ts:139`. It is **verified**.\n- **Scenario:** with no repo override for `paths.sensitive`, `list()` returns `DEFAULTS.paths.sensitive` itself, not a copy. `paths.sensitive.push(CONFIG_FILE)` then appends to the global defaults on every `mergeConfig` call. I called `mergeConfig(undefined)` three times, and `DEFAULTS.paths.sensitive` held three `.gloss/config.json` entries.\n- **Outcome:** `resetConfigCache` and any later `loadConfig` or `mergeConfig` keep growing the array for the life of the process. Every path-glob check then scans an ever-longer list, so the cost grows with each reload. The mutation also leaks across tests and callers that compare against `DEFAULTS`. The fix is to copy the array before pushing.\n\n**2. On the base branch, the change being reviewed supplies its own config** (minor)\n- **Where:** `src/config.ts`, `trustedRef`. It is **read**.\n- **Scenario:** you are on `main` and review `HEAD`, which edits `.gloss/config.json` to reclassify `src/auth/**` as `docs`. `merge-base HEAD main` is `HEAD`, so the config is read from `HEAD`, which includes the change under review.\n- **Outcome:** the \"a change cannot reclassify its own paths\" guarantee does not hold for a commit reviewed from the base branch. Config edits are still flagged `sensitive`, so the exposure is partial. The README describes the merge-base behaviour, but not this case.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES\n\nThe background `npm test` run finished: all 190 tests passed, including the new hostile-config tests. So my earlier note that I had no test result is now settled.\n\nThe suite doesn't catch either defect:\n\n1. **`mergeConfig` mutates `DEFAULTS.paths.sensitive`** (verified). My standalone check still printed 3 for 3 calls, so the array grows on every call and the tests don't check for it.\n2. **A change reviewed from the base branch supplies its own config** (read). No test covers reviewing a config-editing commit while on `main`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
