512dea136d23a88","e53f7e684953d4e6","e5dcf4c676afe9a2","e5f5e95a1ced99d1","e631dcd699b8d8bf","e769ac588e46a4b6","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","e8b4c1dca3c3459d","ea6d1937747e010c","ea9eaaf74db8796b","ebae4cbe7ea22476","ec33a89f3eb1b3e1","ee795f4182eeccc7","f04af5bcb4a53925","f1a5828d279362bd","f32a7d88834a56f2","f72aea690ce12b9f","f7dcb919a01ce51e","f8e2f94934bb7b50","f939839f951a290c","fa29cea540e16e30","fa29cea540e16e30","fade43691fece55d","fc19e811751009b6","fc5a968d53ec2aa2","fd8bddf5946fed04"],"groups":["fec2a20e613652e6","503ed6368d8710f4","f0a78fd508b2f9a9","9aadd502ca3b05d5","5de7f6dd4d07f774","cc61f997d5d8cddd","6c973db6cee8ad63","664acfd686548a0f","9898c2321c8027dc","ca3ca833cde0540e","b18364236cf688c5","832f422e509de37c","a1eeac95e9383cc8"],"findings":"The signals test run is still going, and a Monitor is armed to report when it exits. I'll wait for that before writing up, since the test results decide whether the suite is clean.\n\n## Review of the config-hardening change\n\nI ran the signals suite and three scratch checks; the full `npm test` run did not finish in my session. Two bugs and one missing risk are verified by running code. The rest is from reading.\n\n### Findings, most important first\n\n1. **Bug in `config-sensitive` (verified).** `list()` in `src/config.ts:129` returns the `DEFAULTS` array itself when there is no repo config for that key. `mergeConfig` then calls `paths.sensitive.push(CONFIG_FILE)` on that shared array, so every call adds another copy to the defaults. Running `mergeConfig(undefined)` three times took `DEFAULTS.paths.sensitive` from 6 to 9 entries, with three `.gloss/config.json` entries. The behaviour stays correct because the duplicates are harmless, but the defaults are mutated across calls in one process. The fix is to push onto a copy (`paths.sensitive = [...paths.sensitive, CONFIG_FILE]`). The author's \"always rated sensitive\" claim holds.\n\n2. **Missing risk: the 250 ms budget is per hunk, not per line (verified).** `matchLines` runs one vm call for a whole hunk. A harmless `/(sensitive|secret|token)\\s*[:=]/i` over a 2,000,000-line hunk took 285 ms, which tripped the budget. Every line then counted as a hit. The stated risk only covers a loaded machine. Large generated or vendored diffs, such as lockfiles or fixtures, can also raise the floor on a benign pattern. The README sentence \"takes over 250 ms on a hunk\" describes exactly this behaviour, so it documents a false positive rather than avoiding one.\n\n3. **Missing risk: the skip is process-wide (read).** `skipped` is a module-level set keyed by pattern source and flags. In `show`, `html`, or a multi-commit range, a timeout on one commit makes that pattern match every line of every later commit in the run. The warning does not name the commit, so the cause is hard to find. The author should scope the skip per call, or at least name the commit.\n\n4. **Mismatch: hit ordering changed (read, not run).** The old loop was line-outer, then class, then pattern. The new loop in `src/signals.ts:133-141` is class-outer, then pattern, then line. `signals --json` therefore emits hits in a different order. The author's summary does not mention this, and no test asserts the order. Either note it, or restore the old order by sorting hits by line and class.\n\n5. **Weak test (read; the test passes).** `padding a line cannot switch a trusted pattern off for the other hunks` (`test/signals.test.ts`) asserts `b.js` is sensitive. That holds whether or not `a.js` times out, because `b.js` has its own batch and the skip makes the pattern match everything anyway. The test does not show that padding is harmless, only that fail-closed holds. `padding a line does not hide a hit` is a literal match with no regex stress, so it does not test padding either.\n\n6. **Minor, read.** The comment in `src/safe-match.ts` has a broken line wrap: \"failing open would let a change pad a line to switch a trusted pattern off. Lines are never cut: a\" runs past the 80-column wrap, and `trustedRef`'s comment breaks \"With none of them there / is no trusted base\". Both are cosmetic.\n\n### Grading and annotation check\n\n- **`safe-match` (required):** accurate. The timeout, fail-closed and warning behaviour match the code. The concern is the per-hunk granularity (finding 2) and the process-wide skip (finding 3), not the grade.\n- **`trusted-config` (required):** accurate. `trustedRef` uses full ref names, a working-tree config is ignored, and the no-base warning path is as described. The merge-base anchoring matches the author's own risk note.\n- **`config-sensitive` (supporting):** the behaviour is correct, but the implementation has the mutation bug in finding 1.\n- **`tests` (supporting):** the fixture changes are needed, since the reader no longer sees working-tree config. Two of the new tests are weak (finding 5).\n- **`docs` (supporting):** accurate about where config comes from. It describes the per-hunk budget in a way that hides the false-positive case.\n\n### Removal candidates\n\nNone worth removing. The ordering refactor in `signals.ts` could be deferred, but it is small and the matcher is needed for the budget.\n\n### Simplifications\n\n- Fix finding 1 with a copy instead of `push`.\n- Cap the budget per line, or split large hunks into fixed-size batches, so one large hunk cannot trip the budget.\n- Key the skip per call or per commit, or report the commit in the warning.\n\n### Test results\n\n- `test/signals.test.ts`: 38 of 38 pass, 16.5 s, exit 0 (verified).\n- Full `npm test`: not completed in my session. The background run was cut off, so the other test files (`gloss`, `ary`, `attention`, `carry`, `reviews`) are unverified.\n\nScratch files are in `scratch/chk/` (`defaults.ts`, `budget.ts`, `big.ts`). I made no changes to the repo, so no wip-gloss pass was needed.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
