72aea690ce12b9f","f7dcb919a01ce51e","f8e2f94934bb7b50","f939839f951a290c","fa29cea540e16e30","fa29cea540e16e30","fade43691fece55d","fc19e811751009b6","fc5a968d53ec2aa2","fd8bddf5946fed04","fd9ae7db6499f4b7"],"groups":["ff0074dcc3eebeb3","503ed6368d8710f4","4873f4fc8673de40","9aadd502ca3b05d5","5de7f6dd4d07f774","c32eda83e79b7b5a","6c973db6cee8ad63","664acfd686548a0f","9898c2321c8027dc","bdfe78e989057f13","ca3ca833cde0540e","b18364236cf688c5","832f422e509de37c","a1eeac95e9383cc8"],"findings":"**Verdict on the annotations: mostly accurate, with one real gap.** The suite passes (188/188) and `tsc` is clean in the clone. The main gap is that the config-edit rating can be switched off.\n\n**Mismatches**\n\n1. **config-sensitive (h5), verified.** The task says a config edit is rated sensitive, but that holds only while the base config leaves `paths.sensitive` extendable. With a base config of `{\"paths\":{\"sensitive\":{\"extends\":false,\"globs\":[\"src/other/**\"]}}}`, a change that only edits `.gloss/config.json` comes out as `impact: module` (`attention: look`) and the file's floor is `module`, not `sensitive`. The docs disclose this as \"unless the base config replaces the default sensitive paths\", and the base is trusted by design, so this is a documented caveat rather than an exploit. But the `.gloss/config.json` rule is added to `DEFAULTS` as an ordinary list entry, so it is a default, not a guarantee. A fix would be to append the rule after the merge, outside `extends`. The test \"editing the config is itself flagged\" does not cover this case.\n\n2. **config-sensitive vs TODO.md item 2, read.** TODO item 2 asks for an explicit warning on a config edit. The change does a rating instead and defers the warning. That is a different mechanism from the one TODO specifies, and the summary's \"editing the config is rated sensitive\" under the task is not what TODO item 2 asked for. The author does say this in the summary, so it is disclosed, not hidden.\n\n3. **docs (h1), read.** \"unless the base config replaces the default `sensitive` paths\" is vague. The actual mechanism is `extends:false` on `paths.sensitive`. Naming it would help a reader.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n4. **config-sensitive (h5) is labelled `required`, read.** The task's stated goal is that a change must not lower its own floor. `trusted-config` (h4, h6) meets that alone. The sensitive rating is an addition that makes the config edit visible. It is defensible as part of the task, but \"required\" is overstated. `supporting` or `defensive` fits better, or keep `required` with the note that the rating is a default, not a guarantee (see 1).\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n5. None of the required groups look removable. The `tests` group is correctly `supporting`. The fixture changes (`commitConfig`, dropping `r.commit('config')`, `show HEAD~1`, the stats going from 4 to 3) are needed because the config now comes from the base. They are mechanical, not incidental.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n6. **safe-match (h7–h9), read.** The `skipped` set is keyed on `re.source/re.flags`. The same source in two classes shares one skip, so the second class gets no warning of its own. Keying on class plus source would be more precise, or the limitation could be noted. This is minor.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n7. **Noisy warning on every run, read.** The \"no base branch\" warning prints on any repo whose main branch is not named `main`/`master`, even if it has no config at all. It fires once per process, but for `trunk`/`develop` repos it is on every command. Not listed by the author.\n\n8. **Missing test, verified by probe.** No test covers the `extends:false` path in item 1. Adding one would pin down the caveat the docs describe.\n\n9. **Skip persistence, read.** The `skipped` set lives for the whole process, so one timeout in `show` or `add` across many commits silences that pattern for all later hunks. The author lists this as a risk. Nothing in the output marks it, and `--json` gives no sign. Matches TODO follow-up, so accurate but unmitigated.\n\n10. **Budget on large hunks, verified (no false skip found).** A 300k-line hunk with a configured pattern finished in ~1 s and found the hit, so the 250 ms per-hunk budget did not trigger here. On a loaded machine, a large hunk could still cross it. The author notes wall-clock nondeterminism already.\n\n**Checks run:** `npm test` (188 pass, 0 fail, about 178 s), `npx tsc` (exit 0), plus three probes under `scratch/` (`probe1`–`probe3`) that used the clone's own `bin/git-gloss.js`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
